# Attack-path analysis
Final: low | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Although the logic flaw is real, the default runtime config provisions a single account/credential (main.rs 118-131) and tag management already requires bucket/object ownership (coordinator.rs 3258-3269). The bypass therefore mainly affects self-imposed policy constraints rather than cross-tenant access, reducing impact and likelihood compared to a multi-tenant S3 environment.
## Likelihood
low - Requires valid credentials and a policy that relies on RequestObjectTag; default deployment is single-account and limits exposure.
## Impact
low - Policy-based tag-value restrictions are bypassed for owners, which can undermine tag-based access controls but does not enable cross-tenant access in the default single-account design.
## Assumptions
- Operators use bucket policies with s3:RequestObjectTag conditions to restrict tag values.
- Attackers possess valid SigV4 credentials and can call PutObjectTagging/PutObjectVersionTagging.
- Deployment may expose the HTTP listener beyond localhost via ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR.
- valid SigV4 credentials
- bucket policy with s3:RequestObjectTag conditions
- ability to call PutObjectTagging/PutObjectVersionTagging
## Path
n1 -> n2 -> n3 -> n4 -> n5
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:3432-3460` - PolicyRequest for object actions only includes existing tags; no request tags are added.
- `crates/auth/src/bucket_policy.rs:969-978` - RequestObjectTag conditions read request.request_object_tag_value.
- `crates/auth/src/bucket_policy.rs:332-336` - request_object_tag_value reads from request_object_tags, which are empty in tagging path.
- `crates/auth/src/bucket_policy.rs:1034-1041` - Missing request tag value yields NoMatch for StringEquals.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:3584-3605` - NoMatch falls back to requester_can_manage_object_tags.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:3258-3269` - Only bucket or object owner can manage tags by default.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:118-131` - Single credential record is configured, indicating single-account deployment.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:52-59` - Default listen address is 127.0.0.1:9000 but is configurable.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:138-179` - Server binds to listen_addr and serves HTTP(S).
## Narrative
PutObjectTagging authorization uses bucket_policy_decision_for_object, which only loads existing object tags and never attaches request tags to the PolicyRequest (coordinator.rs 3432-3460). RequestObjectTag conditions are evaluated via request_object_tag_value (bucket_policy.rs 969-977; 332-336), and missing values result in NoMatch (bucket_policy.rs 1034-1041). The policy decision then falls back to requester_can_manage_object_tags (coordinator.rs 3584-3605), which allows bucket/object owners (coordinator.rs 3258-3269). As a result, tagging requests do not enforce RequestObjectTag constraints, enabling owners to set tag values that policies intended to restrict. The HTTP server binds to a configurable listen address (config.rs 52-59; main.rs 138-179), so the path is reachable via the S3 API in normal deployments.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication
- Bucket policy evaluation
- Owner/object-owner tag authorization
## Blindspots
- No runtime PoC executed due to missing ISA-L dependency in the environment.
- Deployment exposure (public vs internal) is operator-configurable and not observable from repo alone.
- Multiple-credential or multi-tenant extensions are not represented in the current configuration.
